Hand DRI3 clients the render node when the primary node cannot be opened.

glamor opens a new descriptor for each DRI3 client on the node the server
drives, the primary node, and authenticates it.  A server that gave up root
(0005) opens with the invoking user's rights, which do not reach the primary
node (0660 root:video) -- so every DRI3 request failed for a desktop started by
an ordinary user.  The render node is the one meant for clients and needs no
authentication; it is opened with the same user rights, so a client gets it
only where the user may open it themselves (the console user, or a member of
`render').  A server started by root opens the primary node as before.

Where the device's description does not name its render node, it is derived
from the primary node's number.  The server says at start which nodes clients
get, and the first time it cannot give a client one it says why: a client's
own message ("could not get DRI3 device") names no reason.

